          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal declarative directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. Its truth or sincerity is separate from its clear avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal words avow a present state but do not undertake future conduct, fidelity, care, or another obligation. Lear rewards the answer with territory, but that response does not convert Goneril's avowal into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled declarative answer in a formal competition for Lear's bounty, not a reflex-like or affective verbal discharge. The surrounding hyperbole does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the utterance functions moderately as strategic praise or flattery in response to Lear's explicitly competitive, reward-linked question. Kent later characterizes the dynamic as power yielding to flattery, supporting this residual persuasive function without replacing the focal avowal's T force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"When power to flattery bows?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal court scene, Lear asks his daughters which loves him most and links their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Goneril answers first with the focal avowal and then elaborates it through extreme comparisons. Lear immediately grants her a territorial share.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril undertakes no definite action or obligation in the exact focal expression. Lear responds favorably and grants her land, so the avowal has successful persuasive uptake, but there is no uptake of a promise. The supplied later conduct strongly undermines the avowal's sincerity, while the words themselves still present love as real.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied source identifies and contains William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The classification concerns only Goneril's marked occurrence of “I love you,” not Cordelia's or Regan's neighboring declarations and not the whole of Goneril's longer speech. Later evidence can inform interpretation of sincerity, but it cannot establish Goneril's private mental state with certainty at the original moment.\"}"
        }
